 "exemplars": {
  "0": [
   {
    "context": "Let's go to Ephesians 2, 8 through 9. For by grace you have been saved through faith, and this is not your own doing. It is the gift of God, not because of works, lest any man should boast. Many Protestants think this verse refutes Catholic views on salvation because they only think about our initial salvation, the moment we go from being a child of wrath to being a child of God.",
    "dist": 0.3438,
    "occurrence_id": 48337,
    "published_at": "2025-12-02T18:45:03Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "grace",
    "t": 2.16,
    "title": "Does Catholicism teach works save you?",
    "video_id": "c2X21fTLfx8"
   },
   {
    "context": "Otherwise, as the same apostle says, grace is no more grace, quoting Romans 11:6. As I noted in my previous episode about Ephesians 2, 8 through 9, the church teaches that we are initially justified by grace alone, apart from faith or works. We simply receive God's gift of saving grace in baptism. There is no work like circumcision, or even a good deed like feeding the poor, that we must do in order to go from being dead in sin to being alive in Christ. The catechism of the Catholic Church says the following Moved by grace, man turns toward God and away from sin, thus accepting forgiveness and righteousness from on high.",
    "dist": 0.3511,
    "occurrence_id": 1574,
    "published_at": "2025-04-30T13:00:05Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "grace",
    "t": 1147.68,
    "title": "Protestants Still Can't Explain James 2",
    "video_id": "0LwEgFig00A"
   },
   {
    "context": "And that is to say that God has elected certain individuals to be saved, and then he gives them saving grace that they cannot resist. They do not have the free will to resist that grace and come to faith. It is unilaterally determined by God. By contrast, Molinists and Arminians will say that the prevenient grace of God brings a person to that point where the effects of sin are annulled enough to allow him to make a free decision as to whether to yield further to the drawing and work of the Holy Spirit or whether to put up resistance against it and reject the gospel.",
    "dist": 0.3792,
    "occurrence_id": 63080,
    "published_at": "2025-04-01T20:15:31Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "grace",
    "t": 2003.92,
    "title": "Does Christianity’s View of Salvation Make Sense? - William Lane Craig Explains",
    "video_id": "nnWzJTAm3N4"
   },
   {
    "context": "We simply receive God's gift of saving grace in baptism. There is no work like circumcision, or even a good deed like feeding the poor, that we must do in order to go from being dead in sin to being alive in Christ. The catechism of the Catholic Church says the following Moved by grace, man turns toward God and away from sin, thus accepting forgiveness and righteousness from on high. Justification is not only the remission of sins, but also the sanctification and renewal of the interior man.",
    "dist": 0.3809,
    "occurrence_id": 1575,
    "published_at": "2025-04-30T13:00:05Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "grace",
    "t": 1161.84,
    "title": "Protestants Still Can't Explain James 2",
    "video_id": "0LwEgFig00A"
   },
   {
    "context": "By contrast, we saw that on the Roman Catholic view, as enunciated at the Council of Trent, both God and man play a role in the process of salvation. First is God's role in providing prevenient grace. Then, second, there is the preparation of the human heart as we respond to that prevenient grace of God. For those who respond affirmatively to God's grace, then thirdly, God infuses justifying grace, so that they are now justified before God by his grace.",
    "dist": 0.3851,
    "occurrence_id": 58296,
    "published_at": "2025-11-09T16:53:58Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "grace",
    "t": 411.56,
    "title": "// Defenders LIVE! //",
    "video_id": "j_5SfRU2zaw"
   }
  ],
  "1": [
   {
    "context": "And I said, listen, we've got to come to terms with understanding who we are. Either we're saved or we're not saved. We're we're we're holy or we're not holy. We're this or that. And what I was trying to say was, hey, as far as grace is concerned, if you're saved, then nothing else matters. If you mess up, you mess up. Just come to terms with the fact that you're either saved or you're not. If you are great, just let everything else go. You don't gotta do, you don't got to jump through hoops. You don't gotta do all this praying, you don't got to do all this fasting, you don't got to do all this Bible study, you don't gotta come to church.",
